00:01:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right to point that out—it’s not just your friends; every news channel leads with “Category X hurricane approaching,” and the entire public conversation revolves around that number. So it feels like it *must* be the measure of how bad a storm will be. Here’s the thing: the Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale (the categories 1–5) technically measures only one thing: **maximum sustained wind speed**. The scale was designed originally to give an estimate of **wind damage** to structures. That’s it. It doesn’t directly measure storm surge, rainfall, tornadoes, or how long the storm will sit over a city. It’s a useful shorthand, but it’s a bit like judging how dangerous a car crash will be by only knowing the car’s top speed—not its weight, not the angle of impact, not whether it will hit a brick wall or a hay bale. Why does everyone use it then? Because it’s simple, and wind speed is a fast, memorable number. But meteorologists and emergency managers have spent the last decade actively trying to de-emphasize the category-only mindset because it can create a false sense of security. They now highlight threats like **storm surge watches, rainfall totals, and the storm’s size and forward speed** because: - **Size matters massively.** Hurricane Irma’s hurricane-force winds extended 80–120 miles from the center; Dorian’s worst winds were in a much tighter core. Irma was a sprawling giant that lashed nearly the entire Florida peninsula, while Dorian’s most ferocious winds never reached the state’s coast. - **Speed kills in a different way.** Dorian stalled over the Bahamas as a Cat 5 for a day and a half, dropping 30+ inches of rain and pounding the same islands with unrelenting wind. That’s catastrophic—but if a storm is racing through quickly, the damage is shorter-lived. Forward speed is not in the category. - **Surge is often the deadliest threat.** Hurricane Katrina was a Cat 3 at landfall in Louisiana, but its core was enormous, and it pushed a 25–30-foot storm surge ashore, killing over 1,800 people. No category number can tell you that. So when news reports say “it’s a Category 4—this is going to be devastating,” they’re making a leap. They’re connecting wind speed with expected destruction based on general experience, but it glosses over everything else. The category number is the headliner, but not the full article. Back to your original question: People might have perceived Dorian as “the worst in decades for Florida” because it was an incredibly violent storm (185 mph winds at peak) that threatened the coast with a direct hit while being a Cat 5. When forecasts had it plowing into Florida at Cat 4 or 5, that fear was very real. But since it never made landfall there, it’s not remembered as a Florida hurricane of impact—the damage was in the Bahamas. Irma, by contrast, was a Cat 4 at the Keys’ landfall and a Cat 3 at Marco Island, but because its entire structure was so large, it tore up the whole state and caused massive power outages, flooding, and wind damage from the Keys to Georgia. Same number? No. Same feel of how “bad” it was? Absolutely. The short answer: the category number is a starting point, not a final verdict. Everyone uses it because it’s the simplest label, but judging a hurricane by its category alone is like judging a book by its cover—it tells you *something*, just not the whole story.
