00:03:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and you’re making a very fair point: when a hurricane hits, it’s the wind that people see tearing things apart, and wind speed absolutely does rip roofs off and knock down power lines. No one’s arguing that wind isn’t a primary destructive force. The misunderstanding is in thinking that the Category number—which is a measurement of *peak* wind speed in a very small area around the eyewall—tells you everything about the wind damage across an entire region. Let’s stick just to wind, since that’s your focus, and I think you’ll see why the category alone can be deceiving, even for wind damage alone. ### 1. It’s not just “how fast,” but “how fast over *how much area*” A compact Category 4 hurricane might have extreme winds only 10–15 miles wide, while a large Category 2 or 3 can spread hurricane-force winds over 100+ miles. So which one will knock down more power lines and rip off more roofs across the state? - **Hurricane Charley (2004)** hit Florida as a Category 4 with strong winds concentrated very close to its center. The worst damage was in a narrow swath; a few miles away, damage was dramatically less. - **Hurricane Irma (2017)** made landfall in the Keys as a Cat 4 and later as a Cat 3 on Marco Island, but its hurricane-force wind field was enormous—over 100 miles wide. It caused wind damage from the Keys to northern Florida, leaving millions without power, tearing up roofs, and felling trees across a huge area. If you only looked at the category, you’d assume Charley must have been more destructive than Irma in Florida, but Irma’s wind damage was far more widespread and, in terms of total impact, greater—because the winds covered so much more real estate. ### 2. Duration: wind that hammers for hours does more damage than wind that passes quickly A fast-moving Cat 5 that blows through in a couple of hours might do less damage than a slower Cat 3 that sits over the same houses for a whole day. Long-duration wind weakens structures, fatigues building materials, and tears away barriers until they fail. Hurricane Dorian’s devastation in the Bahamas wasn’t just because it was a Cat 5—it’s because it *stalled* as a Cat 5, subjecting the islands to those winds for over 30 hours. The category didn’t tell you that. ### 3. Wind damage doesn’t just come from the hurricane itself A hurricane’s wind field also spins up dozens of tornadoes. Hurricane Ivan (2004) spawned 120+ tornadoes that caused damage far inland, yet its category at landfall says nothing about that. Tornadoes are an “extra side factor” that is triggered entirely by the hurricane’s wind circulation and can be more violent than the hurricane’s parent winds. ### 4. So why does Dorian sometimes feel like it was “worse” in people’s minds? Because it was a massive, catastrophic storm—its peak wind speed was 185 mph, it was a solid Cat 5, and for days the forecasted path pointed at Florida as a potential Cat 4 or 5. That fear was very real. But as it turned out, Florida got the outer winds, not the core. Irma, on the other hand, was a weaker storm at landfall (Cat 4 in the Keys, Cat 3 on the mainland), but its core was so large that many more Floridians felt hurricane-force winds, and the damage spanned the entire state. So by the metric of “wind that actually punished Florida,” Irma was worse. The categories alone would have told you the opposite. I get the desire to have a single, simple number that sums up the threat. But even for wind damage alone—ignoring water, surge, and tornadoes—peak wind speed in a tiny ring doesn’t tell you how many roofs will be ripped off across the entire storm’s path. That’s why meteorologists keep hammering on the idea that the category is a starting point, not the full story, even if you only care about wind. It’s not about throwing in “extra side factors”; it’s about the fact that wind damage itself depends on more than just the highest number a dropsonde measured in the eyewall.
