00:03:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a completely understandable reaction, and honestly, it’s a sign you’re thinking about it more clearly than most people. It *should* feel a bit unsettling to realize the simple number we’ve been taught to hang everything on isn’t the full picture. But I want to put your mind at ease a bit, because this doesn’t mean you’re less safe—it actually means the opposite. The category number is still a critical “red flag.” It absolutely does tell you that extremely dangerous winds are coming, and if you’re in the core of a Category 4 or 5, you’ll experience catastrophic wind damage. For that narrow slice of the storm, the category is a great warning. The issue is that hurricanes are big, complex engines, and the edges of that danger zone don’t always match the simple number. So here’s the good news: **the experts who make evacuation orders don’t rely on the category alone either.** Emergency managers use a detailed mapping system called SLOSH (Sea, Lake, and Overland Surges from Hurricanes) that models storm surge down to your exact neighborhood based on the storm’s size, track, and the shape of the coast. They blend that with wind field forecasts, rainfall estimates, and the latest track. When they issue an evacuation order, it’s not because the hurricane is a “Category 4” in the abstract—it’s because your specific area is projected to face life-threatening surge, winds, or flooding from that particular storm, based on much more detailed data. What this means for you: you don’t have to personally decode a hurricane’s true danger from the category number alone. You just need to listen to the local emergency officials. When they say “evacuate zone A,” it’s because they’ve already done the math on all the factors—wind, size, speed, track, surge, rain—and they’re telling you your particular location is in danger, regardless of whether the headline on the news is “Cat 3” or “Cat 4.” In fact, the system has been moving toward messaging like storm