00:04:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent question—you’re touching on the very history of the scale. And you’re right: it was absolutely created to categorize *storm destructiveness*. The confusion comes from how the scale’s purpose has been refined over time, and how “destructiveness” was originally envisioned. The scale was developed in the early 1970s by engineer Herbert Saffir and meteorologist Robert Simpson. Saffir, a wind engineer, designed the 1–5 categories based on the **effect of wind on buildings**, assigning each category a range of wind speeds and describing what kind of structural damage those winds would cause. Simpson, then the director of the National Hurricane Center, added **storm surge ranges** to each category, linking them loosely to wind speed. So originally, the scale *was* presented as a single number that combined wind damage potential with expected surge height. But it didn’t take long for forecasters to realize a critical problem: **storm surge doesn’t correlate reliably with peak wind speed alone.** A large, slow Category 2 could push a higher surge than a small, fast Category 4. The shape of the coastline, the storm’s size, and its forward speed were all huge factors that the simple wind number couldn’t capture. So by the 1990s and 2000s, the storm surge ranges were officially removed from the scale. In 2010, the National Hurricane Center explicitly renamed it the **Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale** and emphasized that it only estimates wind damage potential. Storm surge, rainfall, and tornadoes were explicitly carved out. So to answer your point directly: yes, it was originally intended as a shorthand for overall destructiveness, but it was found to be **incomplete and sometimes misleading** for that purpose, so it was deliberately narrowed to wind only. The public narrative, however, never fully caught up. The scale still gets used as if it’s the old all-in-one destructiveness meter, even though the experts who created it walked that back decades ago. But here’s the important part for your peace of mind: **the scale still does a decent job for wind damage**, exactly the thing you care about. A Category 5 will mean catastrophic wind destruction for anyone unlucky enough to be in the inner core. The "extra factors" I threw in aren’t meant to say the wind scale is useless—they’re meant to explain why two storms with the same number can have totally different impacts overall, and why a Cat 5 can miss a state entirely while a weaker Cat 3 floods and wrecks half of it. The scale is doing its job for wind; it just can’t do a job it was never designed to do for water. So when you hear “Category 4,” it’s still an excellent warning about the wind. But the decision to evacuate is, and should be, based on the fuller picture—which, thanks to that history lesson, the experts now provide separately. You can trust the wind number for wind; just don’t expect it to whisper secrets about the water.
